--------------------------------------------- Login form -----------------------------------------------------


- BUILD A SIMPLE REACTIVE FORM TO AUTHENTICATE OUR USERS.

1️⃣ We would now like to create a login page. New page = New component! Create a component login.


2️⃣ Add ReactiveFormsModule (from @angular/forms) to its imports.
    The template must contain a h1 with the text "Log in".
    The form must contain two fields:
        👉 a mandatory input field named login.
        👉 a mandatory input field named password (with type="password").
    
    To have something not too ugly, use the Bootstrap styles. For example, an email field should
    look like the following:
        
        <div class="mb-3">
            <label for="login" class="form-label">Login</label>
            <input id="login" class="form-control" formControlName="login">
            <!-- error message here -->
        </div>


3️⃣ As the fields are mandatory, we will display an error message under each field. Add a div below
    each input, and 💡make sure it’s only visible when the field is touched and has the error required.
    The message displayed in that div should be "Login is required", or "Password is required",
    with a class invalid-feedback:
        
        <div class="invalid-feedback">Login is required</div>


4️⃣ Copy the following style in styles.css to have a red border around the input when it’s invalid,
    and properly display the error messages:
    styles.css:

        input.ng-touched.ng-invalid {
            border: 1px solid red;
        }

        .invalid-feedback {
            display: block;
        }


5️⃣ Add a button of type submit at the end of the form, containing the text "Log me in!".
    Create a UserService with the method authenticate(login: string, password: string).
    The submission must call a method authenticate(). This method must first check if the form is valid.
    If the form is invalid, mark all fields as touched using markAllAsTouched() and return immediately.
    If the form is valid, this method will delegate to a method authenticate
    (login: string; password: string) of a new UserService. The service method must return an
    Observable<UserModel>, to which the component method subscribes.


6️⃣ Create an interface representing the user, named UserModel. You’ll find the fields of a user in the
API documentation(https://ponyracer.ninja-squad.com/apidoc#resources-user). Add to these fields an
optional token field of type string, that we’ll need later.


7️⃣ In case of success, redirect to the home page. In case of failure, set a boolean signal
    authenticationFailed to true, and use this signal in the template to display an error message.
    This error message should be displayed in a simple div and should contain "Nope, try again":

    login.html:
        <div class="alert alert-danger" role="alert">Nope, try again.</div>


8️⃣ Final step: add the necessary configuration in the router to be able to navigate to this component
    using the URL /login, and replace the "Races" link in the home page by a "Login" link, allowing to
    go to the login page.


9️⃣ Do not forget to copy all the test files and run ng test to be sure that everything is OK.

