CONFUSION:

🤔 In step 5️⃣ i created a user service  with a method authenticate(login: string, password: string)
    that returns an Observable<UserModel>, to which the component method subscribes.
    Now there are multiple ways to subscribe to the observable. 2 ways i can think of right now
    are using toSignal and using .subscribe() (seefrom chapter 17 and 15). So the confusion is which
    is the correct way in this case?

🧠 using .subscribe():
    -> You manually receive values through callbacks.
    -> You manually update component state.
    -> You are responsible for cleanup (unless Angular handles it through takeUntilDestroyed(),
        async pipe, etc.).
        
        private readonly destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);
        this.userService.getUser()
            .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef))
            .subscribe(user => {
                this.user = user;
            });
    
    -> Typical use case: 🔥🔥When you need to perform side effects:

        this.authService.login(credentials).subscribe({
            next: () => this.router.navigate(['/']),
            error: () => this.authenticationFailed.set(true)
        });

        (Notice that you're not trying to expose data to the template. You're performing actions.)


🧠 Using toSignal():
    -> Angular subscribes to the observable for you.
    -> Angular automatically unsubscribes when the component/service is destroyed.
    -> The latest emitted value is stored in a signal.
    -> Typical use case: When the observable represents state that the UI should react to.

        readonly products = toSignal(
            this.productService.getProducts(),
            { initialValue: [] }
        );

        Then:

        @for (product of products(); track product.id) {
            <p>{{ product.name }}</p>
        }

        (No manual subscription required.)


✅ step 7️⃣ talks about side effect. so it's obvious that we should use .sunscribe() method for an
    operation like Login. Bingo!!!!🎇
